# He Has Smeared Soot on Our Faces
Tamil title: முகத்திலே கரி பூசிவிட்டார்

Periyar has neither son nor daughter to render service like the service Maniammai performed in her early days! But he has an elder brother, whose family includes two daughters and three sons. He has an elder sister, whose family includes three daughters and two sons. Before a woman from another family in Vellore came among them, none of these people had failed to take part in serving Periyar or working for the movement.

Two of them, Comrades Gandhi, B.A., and Miranda, B.A., have attended some conferences. They belong to Periyar's own family.

Nor are they alone. There are hundreds of pure-hearted people who would compete with one another to come and undertake the work of protecting Periyar's health.

None of them was needed. Maniammai had to come!

I thought a storm was entering.

For that I was reviled as a scoundrel.

On seeing that lady's rare service, the Dravidian people lost the revulsion they had initially felt.

The situation became one in which the lady would repeatedly say "Father, Father!" to the delight of her own heart, her lips, and the ears of those listening; Periyar would call her "Daughter, Daughter!" in a way that filled listeners with pride and joy; and many thousands who witnessed this scene happily believed that Maniammai was Periyar's foster daughter.

That foster daughter is today Periyar's wife. It was, they say, a registered marriage!

Who, for whatever reason, could call this situation ordinary?

At hundreds of conferences, mothers from our homes proudly pointed Periyar out to the children holding their hands and said, "Look, there is Grandfather. Pay your respects." We heard it and rejoiced!

Pointing to Maniammai as she stood beside him rendering service, they said, "Grandfather's daughter."

And now that grandfather has married the young woman who served him!

"Is it right? Is it proper?" the world asks.

Periyar has quite a number of people close to him, both within the movement and personally, as well as friends.

In every town there are several people who joyfully say, "Periyar discussed such-and-such a matter with me; he asked me to carry out such-and-such a task."

There are many such people in Madras. I too once had that opportunity, though it was severed in the interim.

Despite the presence of so many, Periyar spoke to none of them about this marriage. He sought no advice and gave no hint.

They were needed only for tasks such as buying petrol for the motor car, purchasing paper for the office, and collecting money for the fund.

They were needed only so that he could say, "Hold a conference in this town, at this place, and make the necessary arrangements." Periyar spoke to none of these friends about the marriage.

"I knew nothing! He never told me!" each of them says in astonishment.

We asked former minister Muthiah Mudaliar: "Periyar holds you in great esteem. Did he ever mention it to you? He visited you and dined with you that day; did he tell you then?" In a tearful voice he replied, "I knew nothing! He did not even hint at it!"

When asked, "Arasu, were you aware of it?" C. C. T. Arasu, a prominent member of Periyar's corps of attendants, replies, "Alas, I knew nothing. Periyar told me to remain outside and went alone to speak to the registrar."

When Comrade N. C. Natarajan, who had travelled with him during the recent tour, is asked, "Did you know anything about this?" he sobs, "I knew nothing."

E. V. K. Sampath, his elder brother's son and manager of *Viduthalai*, knew nothing of it.

Comrade Gurusamy, editor of *Viduthalai*, did not know. Treasurer Govindasamy did not know. None of them knew. He spoke to no one about it, not even by the slightest hint.

Yet our leader discussed it with Governor-General Acharya at Tiruvannamalai. He himself says so in his statement. He says, "I spoke to him about a matter that I did not discuss with any of you and about which I had no need to speak to you."

---NOTES---
The visible source begins with a complete title but the last available page appears to end before the article's conclusion. A badly recognised phrase about N. C. Natarajan's recent travel with Periyar was interpreted from the scan and surrounding questions; the Tamil source remains unchanged.
